1
00:00:00,260 --> 00:00:01,836
All right, let's go.

2
00:00:01,920 --> 00:00:02,980
Number 10,

3
00:00:03,120 --> 00:00:04,140
The Door.

4
00:00:04,320 --> 00:00:07,256
The front door of a parlor house
on a cattle town's main street

5
00:00:07,340 --> 00:00:09,596
looked like every other door on the block.

6
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:11,076
Same pine frame,

7
00:00:11,160 --> 00:00:12,516
same iron latch,

8
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:15,700
same coat of paint cracking
in the same prairie sun.

9
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:20,796
A man walking toward it in 1878 could have
been walking toward the dry goods store

10
00:00:20,880 --> 00:00:23,480
or the barber or the land office.

11
00:00:23,620 --> 00:00:26,756
Nothing on the outside announced
what waited on the other side,

12
00:00:26,840 --> 00:00:30,176
but the moment the latch lifted
and the door swung inward,

13
00:00:30,260 --> 00:00:31,636
the world changed.

14
00:00:31,720 --> 00:00:33,216
The air was different.

15
00:00:33,300 --> 00:00:34,416
The light was different.

16
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:35,876
The smell hit first,

17
00:00:35,960 --> 00:00:39,716
before the eyes adjusted, and what it
carried was not what any trail song

18
00:00:39,800 --> 00:00:43,396
or bunkhouse story had prepared
a first-time visitor to absorb.

19
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:45,056
The smell was kerosene.

20
00:00:45,140 --> 00:00:46,576
That was the first layer.

21
00:00:46,660 --> 00:00:47,836
Then lye soap,

22
00:00:47,920 --> 00:00:51,300
the sharp chemical kind used
in the washbasins upstairs.

23
00:00:51,740 --> 00:00:52,916
Then tobacco,

24
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:53,836
not fresh smoke,

25
00:00:53,920 --> 00:00:57,316
but the residue of thousands
of cigars and hand-rolled cigarettes

26
00:00:57,400 --> 00:01:01,436
that had soaked into the wallpaper and the
curtains and the carpet until the fabric

27
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:05,636
itself had become a record of every
man who had stood in this room before.

28
00:01:05,720 --> 00:01:07,020
Then perfume.

29
00:01:07,120 --> 00:01:08,216
Cheap perfume,

30
00:01:08,300 --> 00:01:09,876
floral and aggressive,

31
00:01:09,960 --> 00:01:13,876
layered over something sweet
and sour that had no clean name.

32
00:01:13,960 --> 00:01:17,596
It was the smell of bodies
and business and time,

33
00:01:17,680 --> 00:01:21,716
compressed into a parlor no larger
than a ranch house sitting room.

34
00:01:21,800 --> 00:01:24,056
The piano in the corner was out of tune.

35
00:01:24,140 --> 00:01:25,876
Someone was playing it anyway.

36
00:01:25,960 --> 00:01:28,616
Conversation had been
happening before the door opened.

37
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:31,216
It stopped when the first-
timer stepped inside.

38
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:32,656
The room went quiet.

39
00:01:32,740 --> 00:01:33,896
Not silent.

40
00:01:33,980 --> 00:01:35,576
Quiet in the way a room goes quiet

41
00:01:35,660 --> 00:01:38,556
when every person in it is
now looking at the same thing.

42
00:01:38,640 --> 00:01:41,436
The first-timer stood
in that quiet and understood,

43
00:01:41,520 --> 00:01:42,736
for the first time,

44
00:01:42,820 --> 00:01:44,698
that he was the thing being looked at.

